  "response": "여러 병원을 다니면서 검사를 받아도 원인을 못 찾으면, 몸이 아픈데도 설명을 못 들은 것 같아서 더 답답하셨을 것 같아요.\n\n그리고 '뭘 잘못하고 있는 건지 모르겠다'고 하셨는데, 상담을 권유받으시면 마치 내가 무언가 잘못해서 여기 온 것처럼 느껴지기도 할 것 같아요. 그런데 여기는 잘못한 걸 확인하러 오는 곳이 아니라, 몸이 보내는 신호 뒤에 뭐가 있는지 함께 살펴보는 자리예요.\n\n지금 바로 뭘 이야기해야 할지 정하지 않아도 괜찮아요. 잠이 안 오면 보통 어떤 시간대에, 어떤 생각들이 떠오르는지부터 편하게 말씀해 주셔도 되고요.",
  "reasoning": "The user is a client who came to counseling because they have insomnia and headaches that doctors couldn't find a physical cause for. They were told it's stress-related and referred to counseling. They're feeling confused about what to talk about and seem to feel like they're being told they're doing something wrong.\n\nKey points to address:\n1. They don't know what to talk about - this is common for first-time clients who are referred rather than self-initiating\n2. They've been through multiple medical visits with no answers - that's frustrating\n3. They feel like \"what am I doing wrong?\" - there's a sense of blame or shame\n4. They're not here by choice, they were referred\n\nI should:\n- Validate their confusion about what to talk about\n- Acknowledge the frustration of going through medical visits without getting answers\n- Address the \"what am I doing wrong\" feeling - this is important, they seem to feel blamed\n- Not be overly reassuring or dismissive\n- Keep it natural, warm, and not too long\n- Maybe one question to help move forward, or just a reflective statement that opens space\n\nI want to normalize the confusion of not knowing what to say, acknowledge the physical distress they've been through, and gently reframe the \"what am I doing wrong\" part without being preachy. I should not ask too many questions. Maybe just one gentle one or even no question - just a reflective response that gives them space.\n\nLet me write something that feels natural and warm, not clinical.\n",
